{"fetchedAt":"2026-11-18T23:53:44.365Z","quote":{"name":"Broadcom Inc","symbol":"AVGO","slug":"broadcom","quotes":[{"symbol":"USD","crypto_id":2781,"average_tokenized_price":357.773129777219,"tokenized_market_cap":24660923.68356254,"tokenized_volume_24h":15074516.71472327,"last_updated":"2026-11-18T23:52:01.000Z"}],"rwa_id":13,"asset_type":"stock","rwa_rank":10,"has_tokens":true,"average_tokenized_price":357.773129777219,"tokenized_market_cap":24660923.68356254,"tokenized_volume_24h":15074516.71472327,"last_updated":"2026-11-18T23:53:10.859Z","tokens":[{"symbol":"AVGOX","name":"Broadcom tokenized stock (xStock)","price":357.9264829813545,"crypto_id":37021,"issuer_id":"6878977dcbbf471de3366e85","issuer_name":"Backed Assets","market_cap":7048376.317273138,"volume_24h":1972852.0489445264},{"symbol":"AVGOon","name":"Broadcom Tokenized Stock (Ondo)","price":358.3857346085504,"crypto_id":38062,"issuer_id":"688ca4ccabae9b5b9fb3167a","issuer_name":"Ondo Assets","market_cap":5230129.034506876,"volume_24h":4536904.187043163},{"symbol":"AVGO.D","name":"AVGO tokenized stock (Dinari)","price":null,"crypto_id":37126,"issuer_id":"688c6172abae9b5b9fb30359","issuer_name":"Dinari Assets","market_cap":null,"volume_24h":null},{"symbol":"rAVGO","name":"Broadcom Tokenized Stock (Reality)","price":357.6143893511116,"crypto_id":40604,"issuer_id":"6a43600c282e7240b76c8bbb","issuer_name":"Reality","market_cap":2264894.6107362863,"volume_24h":789830.0481097078},{"symbol":"AVGO","name":"Broadcom Tokenized Stock (Robinhood)","price":357.0451584880127,"crypto_id":40705,"issuer_id":"6a465832fbe3004b1a0ea2dd","issuer_name":"Robinhood","market_cap":218951.82013785816,"volume_24h":863848.6158810065},{"symbol":"AVGOB","name":"Broadcom Tokenized bStocks","price":357.23355602469906,"crypto_id":40845,"issuer_id":"6a2aed5097c45356b1a5f710","issuer_name":"bStocks","market_cap":9456966.430972055,"volume_24h":6346037.410792914},{"symbol":"WAVGOX","name":"Wrapped Broadcom Tokenized stock (xStock)","price":358.8933125565719,"crypto_id":37129,"issuer_id":"6878977dcbbf471de3366e85","issuer_name":"Backed Assets","market_cap":35277.8524115699,"volume_24h":229087.4862256445},{"symbol":"AVGOc","name":"Broadcom Inc. Tokenized Stock (Coinbase)","price":355.7503205022365,"crypto_id":42432,"issuer_id":"6a72fe7072996604d45c6733","issuer_name":"Coinbase","market_cap":390453.4626087454,"volume_24h":335884.2531979348}],"tradfi_markets":[{"exchange":{"slug":"binance","name":"Binance","exchange_id":270},"ticker":"AVGO","market_url":"https://www.binance.com/en/stocks/EQ_AVGO"}]},"info":{"name":"Broadcom Inc","symbol":"AVGO","slug":"broadcom","website":"https://www.broadcom.com","employees":37000,"founded":"1960-12-31","industry":"Semiconductors & Related Devices","cik":"0001730168","about":{"description":"### Who is the CEO of Broadcom?\n\nLeadership signals a company’s long-term priorities, especially in semiconductors where R&D cycles span years.\n\nBroadcom’s CEO is Hock Tan, who has led the company since 2006. Under his leadership, Broadcom has aggressively expanded through acquisitions and pivoted from a pure chipmaker to a diversified infrastructure technology firm.\n\nTan’s focus has been scaling margins through vertical integration and maintaining pricing power in niche markets like custom silicon and enterprise software.\n\n\n### What are Broadcom’s core business segments (e.g., chips, infrastructure software)?\n\nBroadcom’s diversification is often missed by retail investors focused only on semiconductors.\n\nBroadcom operates two primary segments: Semiconductor Solutions and Infrastructure Software. Chips power data centers, telecom, broadband, and wireless devices, while the software arm includes mainframe tools, cybersecurity, and enterprise automation — much of it acquired through deals like CA Technologies and VMware.\n\nThis structure gives Broadcom exposure to both physical infrastructure and recurring software revenue.\n\n\n### What are the key risks of investing in Broadcom?\n\nHigh-margin growth in semiconductors often comes with supply chain and geopolitical risk.\n\nBroadcom is exposed to cyclicality in demand for enterprise and telecom hardware. Its dependence on a few customers for chip revenue (e.g., Apple) poses concentration risk.\n\nThe company also faces regulatory hurdles for acquisitions, and delays can materially impact expansion strategy. Any decline in software renewal rates or chip volumes could affect cash flow.\n\n\n### How does Broadcom plan to stay competitive in the semiconductor industry?\n\nChipmakers can’t rely solely on process improvements — vertical strategy is key.\n\nBroadcom focuses on designing niche, high-value chips where it faces limited competition — such as custom ASICs, network processors, and RF components. It also expands software capabilities to deepen its enterprise stack.\n\nIts acquisition-led model allows it to cross-sell hardware and software while growing its recurring revenue base.\n\n\n### Can I buy tokenized Broadcom stock on the blockchain?\n\nGlobal access to U.S. tech stocks is still fragmented. Tokenization offers an alternative for some users.\n\nYes, tokenized AVGO shares are available on a few crypto-native platforms. These aim to reflect Broadcom’s real-world stock price and can be traded globally with lower minimums.\n\nHowever, tokenized versions are not widely regulated and may not grant dividend or voting rights.\n\n\n### Which platforms offer Broadcom (AVGO) as a tokenized asset?\n\nToken availability depends on whether the platform uses custodial or synthetic models.\n\nSome platforms offering tokenized AVGO include Backed Finance, Swarm Markets, and DeFi protocols like Synthetix. These tokens may track AVGO’s price via oracle feeds or be backed by real shares held in custody.\n\nUser access may be restricted based on jurisdiction, especially for platforms requiring KYC.\n\n\n### How do tokenized AVGO shares work compared to traditional shares?\n\nOwnership rights are not the same, even if price tracking is.\n\nTokenized AVGO shares mirror the performance of Broadcom’s publicly listed stock but are traded on blockchain platforms. In some cases, they are backed by real shares held by a custodian; in others, they use synthetic exposure via oracles.\n\nThey offer faster settlement, 24/7 trading, and global access — but typically do not include dividends or voting.\n\n\n### Is Broadcom involved in blockchain infrastructure or chip development?\n\nThe crypto sector is hungry for hardware that scales. Broadcom’s role remains low-profile but foundational.\n\nBroadcom supplies high-performance chips used in data centers and networking, which can support blockchain infrastructure, though not specifically designed for crypto mining.\n\nIt has not launched any dedicated blockchain silicon or Web3-specific products but indirectly powers cloud and compute systems used in the ecosystem.\n\n\n### How can I invest in Broadcom — stock or tokenized version?\n\nAccess depends on whether you want regulated exposure or decentralized access.\n\nBroadcom trades under AVGO on the NASDAQ and is available via most brokerages. Investors can also access tokenized versions on blockchain platforms, though availability varies by region.\n\nTraditional shares offer dividends and full legal protections. Tokenized shares offer accessibility and lower minimums, but with reduced legal clarity.","logo":null,"website":"https://www.broadcom.com","date_added":"2025-07-18T03:46:38.000Z"},"rwa_id":13,"asset_type":"stock","rwa_rank":10,"has_tokens":true,"primary_exchange":"Nasdaq"},"tokenMeta":{"37021":{"id":37021,"name":"Broadcom tokenized stock (xStock)","symbol":"AVGOX","category":"token","description":"Broadcom tokenized stock (xStock) (AVGOX) is a cryptocurrency and operates on the Solana platform. Broadcom tokenized stock (xStock) has a current supply of 288,298.53404015 with 19,692.24590079 in circulation. The last known price of Broadcom tokenized stock (xStock)